WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.789 --> 00:00:05.730
A lot of the time, it can be weirdly fun to
watch a terrible movie - especially when it's

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:08.290
so bad that it's actually kinda good.

00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:12.747
Some movies, however, are so bad they're just
about unwatchable. Case in point?

00:00:12.747 --> 00:00:15.643
Take a look at these irredeemable flops.

00:00:15.643 --> 00:00:20.750
After the release of his stunning Holocaust
dramatic comedy Life is Beautiful, Roberto

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:23.500
Benigni was the toast of Italian cinema.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:28.980
That movie took home three Oscars and made
millions of dollars at the box office in 1998

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:34.250
- which might just explain why he was given
the OK to film a live-action version of Pinocchio.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:38.680
But it doesn't even come close to explaining
the movie he actually made.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:42.310
Indeed, Benigni's remake was an all-out cinematic
disaster.

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:47.060
The reviews were brutal, the box office returns
were worse, and in a matter of days Benigni's

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:49.920
career in Hollywood was pretty much finished.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.280
But this story does at least have kind of
a happy ending, as Benigni went on to play

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:59.420
Geppetto in the 2019 Italian adaptation of
the classic tale, which performed far better

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:01.745
with both critics and audiences.

00:01:01.745 --> 00:01:07.780
The 2002 action flick Ballistic: Ecks vs.
Sever was arguably the first high-profile

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:13.030
zero-percent whiff in Rotten Tomatoes' then
four-year history—and rightfully so.

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:19.450
Even today, it's hard to imagine how a studio
actually spent $70 million on a convoluted,

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:23.340
far-fetched spy thriller starring Antonio
Banderas and Lucy Liu.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:28.340
Either way, all that money didn't fool critics:
more than 100 of them panned the movie, landing

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:31.270
it quickly in Worst Movie Ever territory.

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:33.179
It's remained there ever since.

00:01:33.179 --> 00:01:38.920
The original Highlander is a compelling contender
for the most enjoyable movie ever made; a

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:42.979
perfectly-tuned epic of '80s cheese, Queen
music, and swordfights.

00:01:42.979 --> 00:01:45.959
Its sequel, however, is its polar opposite.

00:01:45.959 --> 00:01:50.479
Think of it like the cinematic equivalent
of a stage performer accomplishing an incredible

00:01:50.479 --> 00:01:53.763
magic trick and then immediately falling down
a trapdoor.

00:01:53.763 --> 00:01:57.528
"There can be only one."

00:02:03.049 --> 00:02:07.520
Right from the bizarre sci-fi-themed intro
cards, it's clear that something went very

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:09.557
wrong during the conception of this sequel.

00:02:09.557 --> 00:02:14.609
For this movie, the genuinely intriguing concept
of a group of immortals walking the planet

00:02:14.609 --> 00:02:20.170
since the dawn of time has been twisted into
limp speculative fiction about Earth's future.

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:24.450
Even worse, the immortals are revealed not
to be an offshoot of humanity, but rather

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:29.749
a race of aliens from the desert planet Zeist,
directly contradicting the original's entire

00:02:29.749 --> 00:02:32.340
mythology - within the first ten minutes.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:37.169
The result is a movie that pleases nobody;
a betrayal for loyal fans that's still too

00:02:37.169 --> 00:02:39.211
weird for newcomers.

00:02:39.211 --> 00:02:45.540
Within days of its limited theatrical release,
the John Travolta-led John Gotti biopic Gotti

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:49.780
was being confidently hailed as "the worst
mob movie of all time."

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:54.040
While it's hard to be 100 percent certain
if that's actually the case, it's hard to

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.209
see how things could get much worse.

00:02:56.209 --> 00:02:59.230
Gotti's greatest sin is that it's incredibly
boring.

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:04.030
Aside from a few moments of unintended hilarity,
there's just not that much to enjoy.

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:08.219
Bizarrely edited and incredibly difficult
to follow, the movie is essentially a long

00:03:08.219 --> 00:03:13.290
collection of technically uninteresting scenes,
stitched together with voiceover and real-life

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:17.799
archival news footage into something that
only sometimes looks like a film.

00:03:17.799 --> 00:03:21.819
If Gotti ends up being remembered at all,
it will likely be due only to the movie's

00:03:21.819 --> 00:03:24.829
pugilistic and manipulative marketing tactics.

00:03:24.829 --> 00:03:29.629
In an effort to combat its 0 percent favorability
among critics, producers of the film rolled

00:03:29.629 --> 00:03:34.569
out an ad campaign spuriously claiming that
it's not a movie for critics at all, but rather

00:03:34.569 --> 00:03:36.839
something the general public would enjoy.

00:03:36.839 --> 00:03:41.590
And that's fair - if you generally don't think
about movies, talk about movies, or watch

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:45.639
movies, then sure, you might be tricked into
believing this movie is fine.

00:03:45.639 --> 00:03:48.207
As for everybody else - stay far away.

00:03:48.207 --> 00:03:49.537
Capiche?

00:03:50.038 --> 00:03:55.459
Here's the thing about Rotten Tomatoes: movies
that acquire a 0 percent fresh rating aren't

00:03:55.459 --> 00:03:57.780
necessarily the worst movies in the world.

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:02.930
They're all bad to some extent, but they can
just as easily be aggressively average, too.

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:07.790
Stratton is one such movie: Boring and uninspired,
sure, but hey - it's probably not going to

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:09.049
kill anybody, either.

00:04:09.049 --> 00:04:13.449
Coming from the director of Con Air and The
Expendables 2, Stratton is a by-the-numbers

00:04:13.449 --> 00:04:18.933
action movie that never manages to elevate
itself in the way those semi-respectable adventures did.

00:04:18.933 --> 00:04:23.900
The movie follows a government agency's quest
to stop a rogue former Soviet operative from

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:26.320
launching a chemical weapon attack on London.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:30.590
And if that plotline sounds derivative, that's
because it is - and you'd better get used

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:34.450
to it, because there's not an original bone
in this movie's body.

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:38.700
Stratton's cardinal sin, however, is that
it brings together all those cliches and tired

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:43.500
tropes but forgets to throw in any actual
tension or excitement - and you kinda need

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:44.885
those in an action movie.

00:04:44.885 --> 00:04:49.720
So sure, Stratton may not be the worst movie
ever made, but you'd still be hard-pressed

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:52.920
to find a single good reason to watch it.

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:58.560
Eli Roth's 2002 horror flick Cabin Fever is
far more notable for kicking off the filmmaker's

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:03.579
career than it is for its actual merits as
a movie; mostly because it has so few.

00:05:03.579 --> 00:05:08.190
Somewhat obnoxious, gory, and sporadically
entertaining, it's the sort of middling movie

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:12.310
that could have really benefited from the
remake treatment, with another artist pulling

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:16.189
out its best assets while discarding the material
that just doesn't work.

00:05:16.189 --> 00:05:17.823
But that's not what happened.

00:05:17.823 --> 00:05:23.440
Instead, the 2016 version of the movie is
essentially the same film, just with worse

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:26.955
acting, worse casting, and even lower production
values.

00:05:26.955 --> 00:05:32.150
Virtually every one of Cabin Fever's reviews
zeroed in on its utter pointlessness, critiquing

00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:36.440
the movie on a fundamental level and questioning
its very reason to exist.

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:40.680
In a vacuum, this tale of a bunch of pretty
young people getting absolutely wrecked by

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:45.170
a flesh-eating virus probably wouldn't have
warranted such disdain - but considering the

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:49.304
movie's other crippling flaws, it's pretty
hard to know for sure.

00:05:49.304 --> 00:05:54.530
Is there a movie star on Earth more obviously
bored with his career than Bruce Willis?

00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:59.150
Once the very embodiment of the charismatic
everyman, Willis' most recent achievements

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:03.200
seem to have amounted to little more than
tanking the Die Hard franchise and refusing

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:05.310
to appear in The Expendables 3.

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:09.450
But while Willis' more recent projects haven't
exactly been great, it's rare that his movies

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:13.720
get as devastatingly dull as 2016's Precious
Cargo.

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:18.810
Writing for RogerEbert.com, the critic Peter
Sobczynski described this action movie as

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:23.060
a "VOD craptacular" that is basically as forgettable
as they come.

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:27.870
It's a film completely incapable of transporting
the viewer into its world, leaving you constantly

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:31.310
aware that you're watching actors who are
simply going through the motions.

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:35.110
They drive speedboats, they shoot guns, they
collect paychecks.

00:06:35.110 --> 00:06:39.750
There's no artistry here - you know it, the
filmmakers know it, and Bruce Willis clearly

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:41.189
knows it more than anyone.

00:06:41.189 --> 00:06:45.800
If he had just played up to the movie's badness,
Nic Cage style, Willis might have been able

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:49.950
to make Precious Cargo just a little more
entertaining; instead, he looks annoyed to

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:51.389
even have to be there.

00:06:51.389 --> 00:06:57.070
Still, in that sense at least, it's probably
his most relatable performance in years.

00:06:57.070 --> 00:07:02.770
Unfortunately, Simon Sez isn't a movie adaptation
of the hit light-up game - although even watching

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:06.930
a Simon toy light up for an hour and a half
would probably be more entertaining than this

00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:08.770
dire Dennis Rodman sequel.

00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:13.000
The original movie, Double Team, featured
the bad boy basketball player and Jean-Claude

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:15.928
Van Damme teaming up to kick butt and act
badly.

00:07:15.928 --> 00:07:18.699
"Yaz, behind you!"

00:07:18.699 --> 00:07:20.559
"Oh, shh-"

00:07:20.559 --> 00:07:26.070
Van Damme didn't return for the sequel, however,
so they got the next best thing: Dane Cook.

00:07:26.070 --> 00:07:30.870
As such, the movie is filled to the brim with
so-called "comedy," alongside a bunch of shots

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:34.070
of Rodman trying to look cool while things
blow up in the background.

00:07:34.070 --> 00:07:35.830
So, yeah, it's bad.

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:40.050
But it at least serves as an amusing reminder
that Dennis Rodman actually used to be a pretty

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:44.079
big deal… a long, long time ago.

00:07:44.079 --> 00:07:48.520
If you watched the fourth season of Project
Greenlight, The Leisure Class may as well

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:50.670
have been called Schadenfreude: The Movie.

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:55.060
In the reality show about a contest winner
getting a chance to make their own film, Ben

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:59.478
Affleck and Matt Damon picked the supremely
obnoxious Jason Mann as their champion.

00:07:59.478 --> 00:08:04.170
The Leisure Class was the result - a comedy
where nothing funny happens, the characters

00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:08.960
inexplicably change on a whim, and even the
basic story doesn't really make any sense.

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:13.960
On the surface, it's a good idea for a film:
a con man falls in love with his mark only

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:18.010
to have his ne'er-do-well brother come in
at the last minute to mess everything up.

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:22.450
But the tone changes wildly from scene to
scene, the lead is shockingly stiff, and it

00:08:22.450 --> 00:08:24.130
even looks horrible.

00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:29.433
It's truly hard to find anything good about
The Leisure Class, which is why it's so delightful to watch.

00:08:29.433 --> 00:08:34.689
Seeing an arrogant kid like Mann get his comeuppance
in more-or-less real time is more satisfying

00:08:34.689 --> 00:08:37.096
than you can possibly imagine.

00:08:37.096 --> 00:08:42.990
When the sitcom Friends became a hit for NBC
in 1994, Hollywood quickly signed up its six

00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:46.540
young and funny cast members to headline a
number of feature films.

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:50.959
For the most part, these movies were forgettable,
middling romantic comedies, like David Schwimmer's

00:08:50.959 --> 00:08:53.860
The Pallbearer or Matthew Perry's Fools Rush
In.

00:08:53.860 --> 00:08:57.449
These movies were nothing special, but they
weren't exactly horrible, either.

00:08:57.449 --> 00:09:03.899
And then there's Ed, the 1996 sports comedy
starring Matt LeBlanc opposite… a rowdy chimpanzee.

00:09:03.899 --> 00:09:08.670
LeBlanc plays Jack "Deuce" Cooper, a talented
baseball pitcher who chokes under pressure

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:12.300
so much that he's relegated to an obscure
minor league team.

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:17.870
Even more humiliating: the team's third baseman
is its former mascot, a primate named Ed.

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:21.600
Over the course of the movie, Deuce learns
to believe in himself and how to be a good

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:24.980
friend, all because of that wacky chimp with
a heart of gold.

00:09:24.980 --> 00:09:30.980
Not only was Ed a box office bomb, bringing
in a grand total of $4.4 million, but critics

00:09:30.980 --> 00:09:33.930
were united in their utter hatred for the
movie.

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:37.730
For example, Dasson Thomson of the Washington
Post called it:

00:09:37.730 --> 00:09:42.850
"Breathtakingly unadventurous, with its cheesy
story line, hackneyed ballpark-fable sentiments,

00:09:42.850 --> 00:09:47.819
'adorable' animal, and collection of one-dimensional
human nincompoops."

00:09:47.819 --> 00:09:52.930
In the early 1980s, Chuck Norris was the king
of a certain kind of patriotic war movie,

00:09:52.930 --> 00:09:57.769
in which the star could bomb, shoot, and karate
kick bad guys to his heart's content.

00:09:57.769 --> 00:10:02.149
The success of Missing in Action and The Delta
Force would eventually lead to Norris playing

00:10:02.149 --> 00:10:07.100
a mystical, kick-happy lawman on the '90s
action show Walker, Texas Ranger.

00:10:07.100 --> 00:10:12.000
This then led to a revival of his film career,
which by then had taken a bit of a downward turn.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:16.699
This was largely because Norris' style of
movie had grown a little passé - so filmmakers

00:10:16.699 --> 00:10:19.910
tried to open things up by sticking him in
a family comedy.

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:24.470
The result is Top Dog, in which Norris plays
a loose-cannon cop who gets teamed up with

00:10:24.470 --> 00:10:26.519
a super-smart police canine.

00:10:26.519 --> 00:10:31.579
Top Dog is supposed to appeal to kids, but
it's also casually and extremely violent,

00:10:31.579 --> 00:10:34.226
following a plot about extremist terror attacks.

00:10:34.226 --> 00:10:38.769
Critics loathed the movie, too, which wound
up with a 0 percent on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:10:38.769 --> 00:10:42.279
As Roger Ebert asked in his Chicago Sun-Times
review:

00:10:42.279 --> 00:10:46.342
"Why, exactly, would you want to make a movie
about racist hate groups, and then disguise

00:10:46.342 --> 00:10:49.636
it as family entertainment about a cute dog?"

00:10:49.636 --> 00:10:54.620
Launched to stardom in the late 1980s with
the movie I'm Gonna Git You Sucka and then

00:10:54.620 --> 00:10:58.869
in the '90s with the sketch comedy series
In Living Color, filmmaker and actor Keenen

00:10:58.869 --> 00:11:03.730
Ivory Wayans struggled to find a follow-up
project that was a good match for his talents.

00:11:03.730 --> 00:11:08.389
His two stabs at action heroism - Most Wanted
and The Glimmer Man - both bombed… but at

00:11:08.389 --> 00:11:11.600
least critics had a few nice things to say
about those movies.

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:17.356
The same can't be said for Wayans' 1994 romantic
action comedy A Low Down Dirty Shame.

00:11:17.356 --> 00:11:22.149
Wayans wrote and directed the oddly laid-back
movie about Andre Shame, a disgraced police

00:11:22.149 --> 00:11:26.649
detective and private eye who just can't seem
to bust drug kingpin Mendoza.

00:11:26.649 --> 00:11:32.300
Suddenly, Shame finds a new lead, and gets
back on the case with the help of his sassy assistant "Peaches."

00:11:32.300 --> 00:11:36.519
Austin Chronicle critic Alison Macor summed
up the movie best, writing:

00:11:36.519 --> 00:11:40.920
"I'm not sure what type of movie this is supposed
to be or who its audience is, but it's a low

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:45.344
down dirty shame that Wayans' talent is wasted
on this film."

00:11:45.344 --> 00:11:49.449
Some of the movies that have tried and failed
to reach the critical and box office highs

00:11:49.449 --> 00:11:55.828
of the original Jaws include Deep Blue Sea,
the many Sharknado movies, and Jaws' own sequels.

00:11:55.828 --> 00:11:58.800
"You're gonna need a bigger boat."

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:03.029
Making a movie like this is like walking a
tightrope over a proverbial shark tank - if

00:12:03.029 --> 00:12:06.970
it's too violent, salacious, or gory then
it's an outright bad movie.

00:12:06.970 --> 00:12:11.459
If it goes too far in the other direction,
instead focusing on tension and dread, then

00:12:11.459 --> 00:12:14.874
it's usually perceived as little more than
a Jaws rip-off.

00:12:14.874 --> 00:12:17.878
Somehow, Dark Tide manages to be both.

00:12:17.878 --> 00:12:22.350
Halle Berry plays a shark expert and diving
teacher who has to guide a daredevil rich

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:27.569
guy into a part of the ocean so infested with
sharks that it's nicknamed "Shark Alley."

00:12:27.569 --> 00:12:29.740
You can probably guess what happens next.

00:12:29.740 --> 00:12:31.849
As Peter Bradshaw wrote in The Guardian:

00:12:31.849 --> 00:12:36.402
"The sharks themselves are the only ones to
emerge with credit from this."

00:12:36.402 --> 00:12:41.829
Hollywood foisted seven Police Academy movies
onto the world in the '80s and '90s, despite

00:12:41.829 --> 00:12:47.039
only the first being a true blockbuster - and
all of them getting absolutely savaged by the critics.

00:12:47.039 --> 00:12:51.369
As far as Rotten Tomatoes scores are concerned,
Police Academy is among the most critically

00:12:51.369 --> 00:12:54.949
loathed and poorly reviewed franchises in
movie history.

00:12:54.949 --> 00:12:59.930
Entries four through seven - Citizens on Patrol,
Assignment Miami Beach, City Under Siege,

00:12:59.930 --> 00:13:03.930
and Mission to Moscow - are all rated at 0
percent on the site.

00:13:03.930 --> 00:13:08.110
Operating under the premise that a big-city
police department has to sign up virtually

00:13:08.110 --> 00:13:12.839
anyone who wishes to protect and serve, these
particular installments find the cadets training

00:13:12.839 --> 00:13:17.499
new hires, traveling to Miami, fighting an
organized crime ring, and trying to take down

00:13:17.499 --> 00:13:19.194
a money launderer in Moscow.

00:13:19.194 --> 00:13:23.990
Ian Freer of Empire, in his review of Police
Academy 4: Citizens on Patrol, summed up the

00:13:23.990 --> 00:13:27.170
thoughts of his fellow critics with regards
to the whole series.

00:13:27.170 --> 00:13:30.817
He wrote:
"Oh dear... oh dear, oh dear..."

00:13:30.817 --> 00:13:36.300
Tom Selleck got his movie career going in
1987 with the smash hit Three Men and a Baby.

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:40.690
Sadly, he didn't have many more hits beyond
that - although he did offer up a kind of

00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:45.790
reversal on Three Men and a Baby with the
1992 comedy Folks!, in which he played a yuppie

00:13:45.790 --> 00:13:48.579
trying to contend with his aging parents.

00:13:48.579 --> 00:13:54.806
Supposedly a comedy, Folks! is basically a
nasty, 100-minute insult against well-meaning elderly people.

00:13:54.806 --> 00:13:59.560
Selleck plays Jon, who invites his increasingly
infirm parents to move in with him and his

00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:02.199
family after his father burns down their home.

00:14:02.199 --> 00:14:06.439
However, his folks' meddling somehow causes
Selleck's character to lose his job - as well

00:14:06.439 --> 00:14:08.300
as his wife and kids.

00:14:08.300 --> 00:14:11.490
In his review for the Seattle Times, Michael
Upchurch wrote:

00:14:11.490 --> 00:14:16.449
"The film's appeal will depend largely on
whether you feel like laughing at senile dementia."

00:14:16.449 --> 00:14:18.687
And that about sums it up.

00:14:18.687 --> 00:14:23.649
The D-grade E.T. ripoff Mac and Me is noteworthy
for just a few reasons.

00:14:23.649 --> 00:14:28.600
Firstly, it contains perhaps the most egregious
use of product placement in Hollywood history,

00:14:28.600 --> 00:14:33.629
with an entire scene being devoted to an amazing
birthday party held at McDonald's - that even

00:14:33.629 --> 00:14:35.800
features Ronald McDonald himself.

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:41.551
It's also a dance number, which E.T. is notably
lacking, so at least Mac and Me brought something new to the table.

00:14:41.551 --> 00:14:42.959
But that's about it.

00:14:42.959 --> 00:14:47.569
Honestly, the only real reason anyone might
have to watch this silly '80s flick is to

00:14:47.569 --> 00:14:51.910
get some context for a running gag Paul Rudd
has been doing for years.

00:14:51.910 --> 00:14:56.019
Every time he guests on Conan O'Brien's show
to promote a film, he switches out the clip

00:14:56.019 --> 00:14:58.498
of that film with the same clip from Mac and
Me.

00:14:59.819 --> 00:15:04.019
"Eric, stop!"

00:15:08.272 --> 00:15:12.140
He's been doing it for over ten years now,
and to his credit, it's actually a pretty

00:15:12.140 --> 00:15:16.193
good bit - which is more than can be said
for the movie itself.

00:15:16.193 --> 00:15:20.850
Stories about secret government conspiracies
were a big deal in the '90s.

00:15:20.850 --> 00:15:26.060
Unfortunately, for every all-time great like
X-Files or Enemy of the State you had…

00:15:26.060 --> 00:15:28.459
Well, you had something like Shadow Conspiracy.

00:15:28.459 --> 00:15:33.749
Charlie Sheen, at the time a legitimate movie
star after the success of Platoon and Hot

00:15:33.749 --> 00:15:38.569
Shots!, stars as Bobby Bishop, an innocent
and earnest presidential aide who discovers

00:15:38.569 --> 00:15:43.522
a plot orchestrated by the White House Chief
of Staff to assassinate the Commander-in-Chief.

00:15:43.522 --> 00:15:47.970
People get killed left and right as a result,
and it's up to Bobby and a plucky reporter

00:15:47.970 --> 00:15:50.089
to put an end to the threat from within.

00:15:50.089 --> 00:15:54.570
The problem is, Shadow Conspiracy isn't quite
as clever as it thinks it is.

00:15:54.570 --> 00:15:57.069
According to Maitland McDonagh of TV Guide:

00:15:57.069 --> 00:16:01.899
"Corpses pile up, dreadful secrets are uncovered,
and Bishop eventually gets to the bottom of

00:16:01.899 --> 00:16:06.630
things, a full hour after the least attentive
viewer will have figured out who's behind it all."

00:16:07.709 --> 00:16:12.459
In elementary school, millions of American
kids learn about the country's homegrown folklore

00:16:12.459 --> 00:16:19.332
- 19th century "tall tales" about larger-than-life
figures like the lumberjack giant Paul Bunyan and John Henry.

00:16:19.332 --> 00:16:25.528
In the tales, Henry is a Hulk-like, ultra-strong
railroad worker known as "the steel-driving man."

00:16:25.528 --> 00:16:30.779
The 2020 movie John Henry, however, reimagines
the character as a tough, modern-day man who

00:16:30.779 --> 00:16:33.949
uses his abilities to reluctantly right wrongs.

00:16:33.949 --> 00:16:38.179
Terry Crews plays the titular John Henry,
who is trying to get over his violent past

00:16:38.179 --> 00:16:43.647
when he runs afoul of his old crime boss after
helping two Honduran immigrants escape gang violence.

00:16:43.647 --> 00:16:47.970
Movie critics weren't exactly moved by John
Henry, however, collectively bestowing it

00:16:47.970 --> 00:16:50.360
with a 0 percent on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:16:50.360 --> 00:16:52.730
Jeannette Catsoulis of the New York Times
said:

00:16:52.730 --> 00:16:57.850
"No kindness goes unpunished in “John Henry,”
a thug-life thriller so frequently preposterous

00:16:57.850 --> 00:16:59.939
that it almost resembles a parody."

00:16:59.939 --> 00:17:02.870
While Michael Rechtshaffen of the Los Angeles
Times said:

00:17:02.870 --> 00:17:07.865
"John Henry [...] lands with all the subtlety
of the mighty steel-driving man's sledgehammer."

00:17:07.865 --> 00:17:13.290
Flash, fancy cars driven by a rogues' gallery
of cocky, tight-knit characters in a world

00:17:13.290 --> 00:17:17.031
of underground street racing - those are the
elements that helped make the Fast and the

00:17:17.031 --> 00:17:21.750
Furious franchise so irresistible and lucrative
to audiences around the world.

00:17:21.750 --> 00:17:26.040
Obviously, the filmmakers behind Redline hoped
the market was big enough for another movie

00:17:26.040 --> 00:17:30.330
of that ilk, only with less money and fewer
famous cast members.

00:17:30.330 --> 00:17:35.640
Unfortunately, Redline grossed a paltry $8.2
million, largely because the vast majority

00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:41.310
of the moviegoing public immediately recognized
it for what it was: a shameless, unabashed,

00:17:41.310 --> 00:17:44.099
not-very-good ripoff of the Fast and Furious
saga.

00:17:44.099 --> 00:17:45.310
Not convinced?

00:17:45.310 --> 00:17:46.457
Check out this atrocious scene.

00:17:48.940 --> 00:17:52.828
"Eric, stop!"

00:17:56.447 --> 00:17:58.330
OK, we pulled a Rudd.

00:17:58.330 --> 00:18:03.766
But critics loathed the real movie, which
currently sits at an impressive 0 percent on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:18:03.766 --> 00:18:07.700
As J.R. Jones of the Chicago Reader so succinctly
explains:

00:18:07.700 --> 00:18:10.092
"The whole thing is pretty stupid."

00:18:10.092 --> 00:18:13.740
There are a lot of haunted house movies out
there, to say the least.

00:18:13.740 --> 00:18:18.560
It's a tried and true film subgenre, and they
really do fill a niche, mostly because audiences

00:18:18.560 --> 00:18:22.450
love the familiar beats of stories about things
that go bump in the night.

00:18:22.450 --> 00:18:26.780
Now, movie critics traditionally aren't all
that keen on horror movies, but it's still

00:18:26.780 --> 00:18:31.000
pretty hard to make one so bad that it's panned
by literally everybody.

00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:33.850
But that's exactly what happened with The
Disappointments Room.

00:18:33.850 --> 00:18:38.900
In this movie, a woman named Dana and her
family move out of busy Brooklyn in favor

00:18:38.900 --> 00:18:40.420
of a house in the country.

00:18:40.420 --> 00:18:45.960
It's already occupied, however, by secrets,
ghosts, and plenty of foreboding dread, all

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:48.950
thanks to the family that lived there in the
1800s.

00:18:48.950 --> 00:18:53.400
Robert Abele of The Wrap said the movie consists
of "a litany of embarrassments," while Marc

00:18:53.400 --> 00:18:55.800
Savlov of the Austin Chronicle wrote that:

00:18:55.800 --> 00:19:00.457
"The Disappointments Room lives (and dies)
up to its name."

00:19:00.457 --> 00:19:05.600
Conventional wisdom holds that there are two
kinds of successful movies: box office smashes

00:19:05.600 --> 00:19:07.020
and critical darlings.

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:12.030
There actually isn't a lot of disparity between
the two - blockbusters are often praised by

00:19:12.030 --> 00:19:15.900
critics, while award winners can get a serious
financial boost from their plaudits.

00:19:15.900 --> 00:19:21.212
And the movies that critics hate often bomb
at the box office, too - after all, a dud is a dud.

00:19:21.212 --> 00:19:26.300
But then there's Problem Child, a chaotic,
cynical, snotty, and somehow also sentimental

00:19:26.300 --> 00:19:32.550
comedy with a 0 percent Rotten Tomatoes…
that made an absolute fortune at the box office.

00:19:32.550 --> 00:19:37.190
John Ritter, a beloved TV star who couldn't
really get a legitimate movie career going,

00:19:37.190 --> 00:19:42.030
plays sweet Ben, a man desperate to be a father
but unable to have one biologically.

00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:47.300
So he adopts Junior, an almost demonically
bratty kid who makes life horrible for Ben.

00:19:47.300 --> 00:19:49.365
As Dennis King of the Tulsa World wrote:

00:19:49.365 --> 00:19:50.365
"It's misguided.

00:19:50.365 --> 00:19:51.365
It's not funny.

00:19:51.365 --> 00:19:56.030
And in its lame efforts to be irreverent and
hip, it turns out to be just plain mean."

00:19:56.769 --> 00:20:01.500
As noted earlier, Bruce Willis made a name
for himself playing tough guys and reluctant

00:20:01.500 --> 00:20:06.800
heroes in action movies during the 1980s and
1990s, a golden age for the genre.

00:20:06.800 --> 00:20:11.270
It's been a long time since the release of
Die Hard or Armageddon, however, and filmgoers

00:20:11.270 --> 00:20:13.270
have mostly moved on to other things.

00:20:13.270 --> 00:20:18.040
But for a small but devoted audience who still
wants to see the sixty-something action icon

00:20:18.040 --> 00:20:22.560
do his thing, he still makes a few low-budget
action flicks each year - all of which get

00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:25.866
quietly dumped onto home video and streaming
platforms.

00:20:25.866 --> 00:20:30.480
Hard Kill is an especially bad example of
these late-period Willis movies.

00:20:30.480 --> 00:20:36.130
The story of hired guns trying to stop a supervillain
from using computers to cause worldwide chaos,

00:20:36.130 --> 00:20:38.820
filming on this movie took place over just
10 days.

00:20:38.820 --> 00:20:43.300
If nothing else, however, that means Hard
Kill managed to earn its 0 percent Rotten

00:20:43.300 --> 00:20:45.944
Tomatoes score with the utmost efficiency.

00:20:46.393 --> 00:20:51.570
London Fields actually starts out with an
intriguing premise: A psychic named Nicola

00:20:51.570 --> 00:20:54.310
Six predicts that she is about to be killed.

00:20:54.310 --> 00:20:58.250
Her sixth sense tells her that her killer
will be one of three men, so she starts an

00:20:58.250 --> 00:21:02.310
affair with each in order to discover the
true circumstances of her death.

00:21:02.310 --> 00:21:07.150
London Fields is based on a darkly comic,
critically-acclaimed 1989 novel by British

00:21:07.150 --> 00:21:12.670
mystery author Martin Amis, but the adaptation
apparently failed to capture what was so special

00:21:12.670 --> 00:21:13.670
about the book.

00:21:13.670 --> 00:21:18.570
Peter Sobcyznski of RogerEbert.com said that
even viewers who know the novel well will

00:21:18.570 --> 00:21:24.240
have a hard time following the convoluted
action of what amounts to "a boring and garish mess."

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:29.480
David Sexton of the London Evening Standard,
meanwhile, likened London Fields to "a music

00:21:29.480 --> 00:21:32.800
video," while Jeannette Catsoulis of the New
York Times found it:

00:21:32.800 --> 00:21:36.100
"Horrendous - a trashy tortured misfire from
beginning to end."

00:21:36.100 --> 00:21:37.721
Still… it could have been worse.

00:21:38.725 --> 00:21:41.301
"Eric, stop!"

00:21:46.900 --> 00:21:49.370
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:21:49.370 --> 00:21:53.480
Plus, even more Looper videos about the best
and worst movies are coming soon.

00:21:53.480 --> 00:21:57.160
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

